chiaki-ng backport: don't use the Takion send buffer after the receive loop
has ended.

Origin: https://github.com/streetpea/chiaki-ng commit
e5f1dd84d15a81bd12ee399610c030a8e2839c9b ("Don't use the Takion send buffer
after the receive loop has ended", Luke Maj, 22 October 2026; pull request
#852, merge fd41fa07874ac181e1e4f20fe190c1963d11805e), with its regression
test. The code is upstream's, unchanged; only the position of the two new
struct members in takion.h differs, because patch 0004 added members before
them.
Licence of the patched files: LicenseRef-AGPL-3.0-only-OpenSSL (AGPL-3.0-only
with the OpenSSL exception), as in chiaki-ng.

When the receive loop ended (for example on a network error), the Takion
thread finalized the send buffer while another thread (a heartbeat, an IDR
request) could still push a reliable message into it: a lock on a destroyed
mutex, a leaked packet, or a write into the freed packet array. The send
buffer's lifetime is now tracked under its own mutex; once it is gone a push
frees the packet and returns CHIAKI_ERR_DISCONNECTED. The mutex is taken on
reliable (message data) sends only, never for video, audio or feedback
packets. No timing or wire change.

Applies after 0001-0005 to the pin 0e16950165f06e5c3291537c2eeba6e852be7120.

